//? Title: Cubic Hermite interpolation: values, slopes and interval width
//? Subtitle: Actual basis contributions, cubic reproduction and explicit extrapolation
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Construct a curve that passes through sampled values while matching a prescribed
derivative at every node. Four endpoint constraints determine a cubic on each
interval, but arbitrary slopes can overshoot and queries outside the node range
require an explicit policy. This report follows actual basis contributions on a
two-unit interval, retains the original four-node examples, and checks cubic
reproduction and a quartic error identity. It distinguishes physical dy/dx
slopes from normalized-coordinate derivatives, explains why the original tangent
example stays bounded, and gives a separate genuine overshoot witness. The
library extrapolates with its nearest end cubic rather than clamping queries.
)

\toc

> A two-unit interval, checked by hand

Take nodes x=2,4, values y=1,3 and physical slopes 2,−1. At query x=3 the width
is h=2 and t=1/2. The four basis values are 1/2,1/8,1/2,−1/8. After multiplying
the tangent terms by h, their four contributions are 1/2,1/2,3/2,1/4, summing to
2.75. Omitting the width factors would impose different physical slopes.

The native trace below captures the selected interval, normalized coordinate,
basis and weighted terms from the value-producing evaluation itself. The concept
diagram plots the actual segment with its endpoint tangent directions.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hermite_terms.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/hermite.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <vector>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const std::vector<double> xs{2, 4}, ys{1, 3}, ms{2, -1};
    const auto e = dm::algorithms::hermite_evaluate(xs, ys, ms, 3);
    std::print("| term | actual basis | actual weighted contribution |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < 4; ++j)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", j, e.basis[j], e.terms[j]);
    std::print("\nSelected interval {}; width {}; t {}; actual value {}; extrapolated {}.\n",
               e.interval, e.width, e.t, e.value, e.extrapolated);
    std::vector<double> q;
    for (int j = 0; j <= 100; ++j)
        q.push_back(2 + j / 50.);
    const auto y = dm::algorithms::hermite_evaluate_many(xs, ys, ms, q);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d(160, 1000);
    dm::graphics::DiagramStroke curve;
    curve.color = {37, 99, 235};
    curve.width = 3;
    std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> points;
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < q.size(); ++j)
        points.push_back({q[j], y[j]});
    d.path(points, curve);
    dm::graphics::DiagramStroke tangent;
    tangent.color = {220, 38, 38};
    tangent.width = 2;
    tangent.dashed = true;
    d.path({{1.8, .6}, {2.4, 1.8}}, tangent).path({{3.6, 3.4}, {4.3, 2.7}},
                                                  tangent);
    d.ellipse({2, 1}, .04, .04, {15, 23, 42}).ellipse({4, 3}, .04, .04,
                                                      {15, 23, 42}).ellipse(
        {3, e.value}, .04, .04, {5, 150, 105}).text({2, .3}, "(2,1), slope 2",
                                                    22).text({4, 3.8},
                                                             "(4,3), slope −1",
                                                             22).text(
        {3.7, 2.0}, "p(3) = 2.75", 22).text({3, 4.6},
                                            "Width h = 2 rescales physical slopes",
                                            25).save_svg(
        "build/hermite_terms.svg");
    if (e.value != 2.75 || e.terms != std::array<double, 4>{.5, .5, 1.5, .25})
        throw std::runtime_error("basis terms");
}
```
