\caption(Equal coordinate scales show the prescribed physical tangent directions. The midpoint uses actual weighted terms, including width factors.)
\alttext(A cubic joins values 1 and 3 at x=2 and x=4 with slopes 2 and minus 1; a marked midpoint has value 2.75 and dashed endpoint tangents show its shape.)

> Derive the four basis functions and uniqueness

Write the normalized cubic as P(t)=a+bt+ct²+dt³. Its four constraints are
P(0)=y0, P(1)=y1, P′(0)=hm0 and P′(1)=hm1, since d/dx=(1/h)d/dt. Solving these
four linear equations gives

$$p(x)=h_{00}(t)y_0+h_{10}(t)hm_0+h_{01}(t)y_1+h_{11}(t)hm_1,\qquad t=\frac{x-x_0}{h},$$
\alttext(The cubic is a weighted sum of endpoint values and interval-width-scaled physical derivatives.)

$$h_{00}=2t^3-3t^2+1,\quad h_{10}=t^3-2t^2+t,\quad h_{01}=-2t^3+3t^2,\quad h_{11}=t^3-t^2.$$
\alttext(Four cubic basis polynomials separately select the two endpoint values and the two endpoint normalized derivatives.)

For example, h00 has value 1 at t=0 and value 0 at t=1, with derivative zero at
both ends; h10 has derivative 1 at t=0 and all three other endpoint constraints
zero. The remaining two basis functions exchange endpoint roles. A difference
between two candidate cubics would have double zeros at both endpoints and
therefore be divisible by t²(t−1)². Its degree is at most 3, so it must vanish
identically. This proves uniqueness and exact-arithmetic reproduction of any
cubic with its correct endpoint derivatives.

Sharing one slope per node makes adjacent segments agree in value and first
derivative: the piecewise curve is C1. Its second derivatives can jump, and
arbitrary slopes do not guarantee monotonicity or no overshoot. Floating
evaluations approximate these identities; they are not symbolic certificates or
interval bounds.

> Retain the original four-node examples

The original zig-zag nodes are x=(0,1,2,3), y=(0,1,0,1). Zero slopes give flat
tangents at every node. The original finite-difference choice is (1,0,0,1):
one-sided secants at the endpoints and centered differences inside this uniform
grid. This is a specified slope rule, rather than a claim about every endpoint
convention used for Catmull–Rom curves.

Both resulting curves stay in [0,1] here. On the first interval the
finite-difference curve is −t³+t²+t, whose derivative is nonnegative on [0,1];
the middle uses a reversed smoothstep, and the last is −t³+2t² with nonnegative
derivative. The old report's overshoot assertion for this particular fixture was
incorrect. A sampled range check accompanies that algebraic conclusion below.

The same program retains the original independent cubic g(x)=x³−2x+1, with
analytic slopes 3x²−2, and compares its values at 0.5,1.5,2.5. Cubic
reproduction is a mathematical identity, while the printed differences are
native floating diagnostics.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hermite_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/hermite.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <vector>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const std::vector<double> xs{0, 1, 2, 3}, ys{0, 1, 0, 1}, zero(4, 0), centered{1, 0, 0, 1};
    std::vector<double> q;
    for (int j = 0; j <= 300; ++j)
        q.push_back(j / 100.);
    const auto a = dm::algorithms::hermite_evaluate_many(xs, ys, zero, q);
    const auto b = dm::algorithms::hermite_evaluate_many(xs, ys, centered, q);
    const auto[amin, amax] = std::minmax_element(a.begin(), a.end());
    const auto[bmin, bmax] = std::minmax_element(b.begin(), b.end());
    std::print("Original sampled ranges: zero [{},{}]; finite differences [{},{}].\n",
               *amin, *amax, *bmin, *bmax);
    std::vector<double> gy, gm;
    for (double x : xs) {
        gy.push_back(x * x * x - 2 * x + 1);
        gm.push_back(3 * x * x - 2);
    }
    std::print("\n| query | native cubic interpolation | cubic reference | difference |\n| ---: | ---: | " "---: | ---: |\n");
    for (double x : {.5, 1.5, 2.5}) {
        const auto e = dm::algorithms::hermite_evaluate(xs, gy, gm, x);
        const double expected = x * x * x - 2 * x + 1;
        std::print("| {} | {:.17g} | {:.17g} | {:.3g} |\n", x, e.value,
                   expected, e.value - expected);
        if (std::abs(e.value - expected) > 1e-12)
            throw std::runtime_error("cubic reproduction");
    }
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.line(q, a, "zero slopes", {5, 150, 105}, 2.).line(
        q, b, "original finite differences", {220, 38, 38}, 2.).points(
        xs, ys, "nodes", {37, 99, 235}, 7.).title("Original zig-zag: different slopes, neither curve overshoots").x_label("x").y_label("interpolated y").y_limits(-.2, 1.6).save_svg("build/hermite_original.svg");
    if (*amin < 0 || *amax > 1 || *bmin < 0 || *bmax > 1)
        throw std::runtime_error("original range");
}
```
