\caption(Actual batch evaluations retain the original points and two slope choices. The algebraic derivative signs establish boundedness; a finite grid alone would not prove it.)
\alttext(Two curves share four zig-zag nodes in [0,1]; both remain within that range, with distinct endpoint tangent shapes.)

> Check the fourth-order remainder without invented error floors

For f with a continuous fourth derivative and exact endpoint values and slopes,
the local error for a query inside[a,b] is

$$f(x)-p(x)=\frac{f^{(4)}(\xi)}{4!}(x-a)^2(x-b)^2,\qquad |f(x)-p(x)|\le\frac{h^4}{384}\max_{[a,b]}|f^{(4)}|.$$
\alttext(The two-node Hermite remainder has double endpoint zeros; its maximum product inside the interval is h to the fourth over 16.)

A proof uses repeated Rolle arguments on f−p minus a multiple of (x−a)²(x−b)²,
choosing the multiple to make an additional zero at x. Its fourth derivative
then vanishes somewhere in the interval. The double zeros encode both value and
derivative matching. This needs smoothness and accurate supplied derivatives; it
is not a guarantee for arbitrary slope estimates or extrapolation.

For f(x)=x⁴ on [0,h], f−p=x²(x−h)² exactly. At x=h/2 the error is h⁴/16. Binary
widths and dyadic query points make the short sequence below exactly
representable, so the program checks that identity without logarithmically
clamping zero differences.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hermite_error.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/hermite.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <vector>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    std::vector<double> kx, actual, reference;
    std::print("| refinement k | interval width | actual midpoint error | h^4/16 |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | " "---: |\n");
    for (int k = 0; k <= 10; ++k) {
        const double h = std::ldexp(1., -k), h4 = h * h * h * h;
        const auto e =
            dm::algorithms::hermite_evaluate({0, h}, {0, h4},
                                             {0, 4 * h * h * h}, h / 2);
        const double truth =
            std::pow(h / 2, 4), error = truth - e.value, expected = h4 / 16;
        std::print("| {} | {:.12g} | {:.12g} | {:.12g} |\n", k, h, error,
                   expected);
        if (error != expected || error <= 0)
            throw std::runtime_error("quartic remainder");
        kx.push_back(k);
        actual.push_back(std::log2(error));
        reference.push_back(-4 * k - 4);
    }
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.line(kx, reference, "exact quartic identity", {5, 150, 105}, 3.).points(kx, actual, "actual midpoint error", {37, 99, 235}, 6.).title("Quartic fixture: halving width reduces error by 16").x_label("refinement k, h = 2^(-k)").y_label("log2 positive midpoint error").y_limits(-48, 8).save_svg("build/hermite_error.svg");
}
```
