\alttext(A straight line decreases by four on the base-two error scale each refinement, matching error reduction by 16.)

> A genuine overshoot and an explicit extrapolation policy

On[0,1] with values 0,1 and slopes 8,0, the cubic is p(t)=6t³−13t²+8t. Its
derivative vanishes at t=4/9 and 1, and p(4/9)=368/243>1. This is an actual
overshoot witness from the same API. No automatic slope limiter is applied.

Queries outside the complete node range use the nearest end interval but retain
their original x, hence t can be below 0 or above 1. For the zero-slope unit
interval p(x)=3x²−2x³, querying x=2 gives −4, not the endpoint value 1. The next
block prints that policy and checks shape, finite-domain and batch-output
limits.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hermite_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/hermite.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <vector>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    std::vector<double> q;
    for (int j = 0; j <= 200; ++j)
        q.push_back(j / 200.);
    const auto values =
        dm::algorithms::hermite_evaluate_many({0, 1}, {0, 1}, {8, 0}, q);
    const auto peak =
        dm::algorithms::hermite_evaluate({0, 1}, {0, 1}, {8, 0}, 4. / 9);
    const auto outside =
        dm::algorithms::hermite_evaluate({0, 1}, {0, 1}, {0, 0}, 2);
    std::print("Actual overshoot p(4/9)={:.15g}; rational reference {:.15g}. Extrapolated x={} selects " "interval {}, t={}, value={}, flag {}.\n",
               peak.value, 368. / 243, outside.x, outside.interval, outside.t,
               outside.value, outside.extrapolated);
    bool duplicate = false, cap = false, nan = false;
    try {
        (void) dm::algorithms::hermite_interpolate({0, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, 0}, 0);
    } catch (const std::invalid_argument &) {
        duplicate = true;
    }
    try {
        (void) dm::algorithms::hermite_evaluate_many({0, 1}, {0, 1}, {0, 0},
                                                     {0, .5, 1}, 2);
    } catch (const std::length_error &) {
        cap = true;
    }
    try {
        (void) dm::algorithms::hermite_evaluate_many({0, 1}, {0, 1}, {0, 0},
                                                     {0,
                                                      std::numeric_limits<
                                                      double>::quiet_NaN()}, 1);
    } catch (const std::invalid_argument &) {
        nan = true;
    }
    const auto exact =
        dm::algorithms::hermite_evaluate_many({0, 1}, {0, 1}, {0, 0},
                                              {0, .5, 1}, 3);
    std::print("Duplicate nodes rejected {}; one-short query cap rejects {}; nonfinite query rejected " "before cap {}; exact cap returns {} values.\n",
               duplicate, cap, nan, exact.size());
    if (!duplicate || !cap || !nan || outside.value != -4 || peak.value <= 1)
        throw std::runtime_error("controls");
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.line(q, values, "slopes 8,0", {220, 38, 38}, 2.).line(
        {0., 1.}, {1., 1.}, "endpoint maximum", {148, 163, 184}, 1.5).points(
        {4. / 9}, {peak.value}, "actual stationary point", {37, 99, 235}, 7.).title("Arbitrary physical slopes can overshoot endpoint values").x_label("x in [0,1]").y_label("interpolated y").y_limits(-.1, 2.2).save_svg("build/hermite_controls.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Actual overshoot p(4/9)=1.51440329218107; rational reference 1.51440329218107. Extrapolated x=2 selects interval 0, t=2, value=-4, flag true.
Duplicate nodes rejected true; one-short query cap rejects true; nonfinite query rejected before cap true; exact cap returns 3 values.
\image(figures/hermite_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The in-range overshoot is real. The separate x=2 result illustrates unbounded end-cubic extrapolation, rather than a clamped interpolation policy.)
\alttext(A red cubic rises above the horizontal endpoint maximum 1 to about 1.514 at 4/9, then returns to the value 1 at the right endpoint.)

All entry points validate at least two strictly increasing finite nodes and
equal-sized finite value/slope arrays. Repeated or unordered nodes and nonfinite
queries are invalid. Finite endpoints can still have an overflowing width;
normalized coordinates, basis terms, weighted contributions or final sums can
overflow even with otherwise finite inputs. Such arithmetic throws rather than
returning NaN or infinity. Input arrays are never sorted, clamped or mutated by
these operations.

The scalar and fixed-size trace APIs validate all n nodes for each call, then
select an interval with O(log n) lookup; total cost is O(n+log n), constant
auxiliary storage and no heap allocation. Traces own fixed scalar arrays, need
no history cap and retain no input references. `hermite_evaluate_many` validates
nodes once and all q queries before any optional cap/allocation, then uses the
same producing kernel for O(n+q+q log n) work and O(q) output. Query order is
preserved, including repeats and extrapolation. The default complete output cap
is one million values, zero disabling that policy; it is not bytes, peak memory
or an input-node cap. Empty batches still validate nodes. No partial output
returns on failure, and independent calls share no mutable state.

Legacy `hermite_interpolate` remains available from `interpolation_extra.hpp`
and preserves ordinary finite output arithmetic and end-interval policy. The
other interpolation algorithms in that module retain their own contracts; this
report does not change their validation or slope rules.

> What the examples establish

Matching values and physical slopes determines a unique cubic per interval.
Width factors connect the basis to derivative units, and shared nodal slopes
ensure C1 joins in exact arithmetic. The original cubic is reproduced, the
quartic fixture isolates fourth-order local error, and a separate counterexample
proves that unrestricted slopes can overshoot. Extrapolation, derivative error
and floating arithmetic require explicit limits. An unrelated dataset would not
strengthen these controlled numerical checks.

The canonical reference is [NIST DLMF §3.3(vi), Other Interpolation
Methods](https://dlmf.nist.gov/3.3#vi), which identifies Hermite interpolation
and its references; this report derives the two-endpoint basis, uniqueness and
local remainder directly. The implemented arithmetic is the library's floating
basis evaluation, not a symbolic or interval method.

Verification compares ordinary legacy results with a frozen implementation,
exercises 625 cubic coefficient combinations on irregular nodes under
sanitizers, and checks the dyadic quartic identity, original bounded curves,
genuine overshoot and extrapolation. An independent rational polynomial solves
the four endpoint constraints without reusing the native basis formula. API
examples, complete programs, inspected figures and both exports are verified
before retiring the original report.
